 corresponding cyclic intervals must overlap. Given \\( k \\leq n/2 \\), an interval of length \\( k \\) cannot cover more than half the circle, ensuring that any two overlapping intervals must share at least one common element or wrap around to intersect.\n\n3. **Element Frequency**: Each element \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\) appears exactly \\( k \\) times across all subsequences. Thus, any set of pairwise intersecting subsequences must share elements, with each element's frequency bounding the maximum clique size.\n\n4. **Maximal Clique via Common Element**: Fix an element \\( x \\). There are exactly \\( k \\) subsequences containing \\( x \\), forming a clique of size \\( k \\). Any larger clique would require exceeding this element's frequency, violating the pigeonhole principle.\n\n5. **Structural Constraints**: Without a common element, intervals must overlap consecutively around the circle. However, the minimal spacing (due to \\( k \\)-step progression) and interval length \\( k \\) restrict such configurations to at most \\( k \\) intervals, preventing overlaps beyond this bound.\n\n**Conclusion**: Whether through shared elements or cyclic overlap patterns, the maximum number of pairwise intersecting subsequences is constrained to \\( k \\). Thus, among \\( B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_n \\), there are indeed at most \\( k \\) subsequences with non-empty pairwise intersections.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{k}", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nGiven a sequence \\(1, 2, \\cdots, n, 1, 2, \\cdots, n, \\cdots, 1, 2, \\cdots, n\\) which is repeated \\(k\\) times, split this sequence into \\(n\\) subsequences \\(B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_n\\) each containing \\(k\\) elements. Verify that at most \\(k\\) of these subsequences can be non-empty when intersected pairwise.\n\n\n1. **Considering the structure of the sequence:**\n   The sequence:\n   \\[\n   1, 2, \\cdots, n, 1, 2, \\cdots, n, \\cdots, 1, 2, \\cdots, n,\n   \\]\n   repeated \\(k\\) times gives a total of \\(nk\\) terms, each integer from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) repeating \\(k\\) times.\n   \n2. **Constraints on \\(k\\):**\n   Given \\(k \\leq \\frac{n}{2}\\), we need to ensure that when forming subsequences \\(B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_n\\), any two subsequences do not overlap more than \\(k\\) times.\n\n3. **Forming the subsequences:**\n   We form subsequences \\(B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_n\\) such that the first occurrence of each integer from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) will form \\(B_1\\), the next occurrence will contribute to \\(B_2\\), and so on.\n\n4. **Considering intersections:**\n   Each number appears exactly \\(k\\) times. So, a value \\(1\\) will be in \\(k\\) distinct subsequences, spread out across the sequence. If we pick \\(k+1\\) subsequences, there will definitely be at least two subsequences \\(B_i\\) that do not intersect.\n\n5. **Application of the Pigeonhole Principle:**\n   By the **Pigeonhole Principle**, if we partition \\(k+1\\) elements into \\(k\\) bins, at least one bin must contain more than one element. Here, each subsequence accounts for a bin.\n\n6. **Structuring subsequences:**\n   According to the Pigeonhole Principle, it implies that if the sequence is split into subsequences \\(B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_n\\), splitting any \\(k+1\\) elements must violate the non-overlapping requirement because there would be \\(l+1\\) elements in \\(m\\) groups.\n\n   - Let \\((k, n) = m\\), and let \\(l = \\frac{k}{m}\\).\n   - Since \\(k \\mid ln\\), the first \\(l\\) groups \\(\\{1, 2, \\cdots, n\\}\\) form \\( \\ln \\), breaking into \\(\\frac{ln}{k} = \\frac{n}{m} = s\\) subsequences:\n     \\[\n     B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_s; B_{s+1}, \\cdots, B_{2s}; \\cdots; B_{(m-1)s+1}, \\cdots, B_n,\n     \\]\n     \\(s\\) subsequences not intersecting with each.\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n   When choosing any \\(k+1\\) subsequences \\(B_1, B_2, \\cdots, B_n\\) among the \\(n\\) subsequences, at least two of them will not intersect (i.e., they will be non-empty when paired).\n\n\\boxed{\\tex