's any other number possible. Suppose the number is 16. Let's see:\n\nIf number is 16:\n\nQian's statements: perfect square (true), <5 (false). So Qian is M.\n\nDai's statements: first part true (perfect square), second part true (Qian's second statement is wrong). So Dai is T.\n\nLu's statements: <7 (false), two-digit (true). So Lu is M. But this would mean two Ms (Qian and Lu), which is invalid because roles must be one T, one F, one M. Hence, 16 is invalid.\n\nSimilarly for 25: Qian M, Dai T, Lu's statements: <7 (false), two-digit (true) \u2192 Lu M. Again two Ms. Not allowed.\n\nThus, the only possible number is 9.\n\nWait, but let's check if there's any other way to assign roles. For example, what if Dai is M?\n\nSuppose Dai is M. Then one of his statements is true and one false. Let's see:\n\nIf Dai's first statement is true (Qian's first part correct) and second is false (Qian's second part is correct, i.e., number <5). So:\n\nDai first true: Qian's first part correct \u2192 number is perfect square.\n\nDai second false: So Qian's second part is correct \u2192 number <5.\n\nTherefore, number is perfect square and <5 \u2192 1,4. Then Qian's statements are both true \u2192 Qian T. Then Lu must be F or M. If Qian T, Dai M, then Lu must be F. Lu's statements: <7 (true if number is 1 or 4) and two-digit (false). So Lu would be M (one true, one false). But we already have Dai as M, so two Ms again. Invalid.\n\nAlternatively, Dai's first statement false (Qian's first part wrong) and second statement true (Qian's second part wrong). So:\n\nDai first false: number is not a perfect square.\n\nDai second true: Qian's second statement is wrong \u2192 number >=5.\n\nThus, number is not a perfect square and >=5.\n\nIf Dai is M, then someone else must be T and F.\n\nSuppose Qian is F (both statements false). So number is not a perfect square and >=5 (same as Dai's analysis). But if Dai's first statement is false (Qian's first part wrong), which aligns. Then Qian's statement