uation.\n\nGiven that sum of squares equals sum of pairwise products. Let's think in terms of variables.\n\nLet me consider writing the equation as:\n\na\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 + d\u00b2 + e\u00b2 - (ab + ac + ad + ae + bc + bd + be + cd + ce + de) = 0\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n(1/2)[(a - b)^2 + (a - c)^2 + (a - d)^2 + (a - e)^2 + (b - c)^2 + (b - d)^2 + (b - e)^2 + (c - d)^2 + (c - e)^2 + (d - e)^2] = 0\n\nWait, is this correct? Let me check. The sum of all pairwise products is equal to (sum of variables)^2 - sum of squares divided by 2. Wait, maybe another approach.\n\nAlternatively, the left-hand side (LHS) is sum of squares minus sum of pairwise products. Let's note that:\n\nSum_{i < j} (a_i - a_j)^2 = (n-1)Sum a_i^2 - 2 Sum_{i < j} a_i a_j\n\nFor n variables. Here, n = 5. Therefore,\n\nSum_{i < j} (a_i - a_j)^2 = 4(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 + d\u00b2 + e\u00b2) - 2(ab + ac + ... + de)\n\nBut according to the given condition, sum of squares = sum of pairwise products. Let's denote Q = sum of squares, P = sum of pairwise products. Then Q = P.\n\nTherefore, Sum_{i < j} (a_i - a_j)^2 = 4Q - 2P = 4Q - 2Q = 2Q\n\nBut Q is positive because all numbers are positive. Therefore, Sum_{i < j} (a_i - a_j)^2 = 2Q. But since all terms are squares, this is non-negative, which makes sense.\n\nHmm, not sure if this helps yet.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps think of variables arranged in some order. Let me assume a < b < c < d < e. Then, since they are distinct, all differences are positive.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach. Let me consider the equation:\n\nsum_{i=1}^5 a_i^2 = sum_{1 <= i < j <=5} a_i a_j\n\nLet me denote S = a + b + c + d + e. Then, sum of pairwise products is [S\u00b2 - sum a_i^2]/2. So substituting into the equation:\n\nsum a_i^2 = [S\u00b2 - sum a_i^2]/2\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n2 sum a_i^2 = S\u00b2 - sum a_i^2\n\nTherefore, 3 sum a_i^2 = S\u00b2\n\nSo 3(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 + d\u00b2 + e\u00b2) = (a + b + c + d + e)^2\n\nAh, this is a k