e Gergonne point is the concurrence of the lines from the vertices to the in-touch points. Hmm. Maybe the Nagel point is involved here. But how?\n\nAlternatively, since A1 is the foot of the altitude, maybe there is some relation between the orthocenter and the Nagel point. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that in a tangential quadrilateral (since B1C1 is tangent to the incircle), there are certain properties. Wait, B1C1 is tangent to the incircle. So, the incircle is tangent to B1C1 at some point, say T. Then, IT is perpendicular to B1C1, and IT = r, the inradius.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the condition for a line to be tangent to a circle. If B1C1 is tangent to the incircle, then the distance from the inradius center I to B1C1 is equal to r. So, the distance from I to B1C1 is r. So, maybe we can write an equation for this distance and get a relation between the sides.\n\nAlternatively, in terms of coordinates. Let me try that approach.\n\nLet me set up coordinate system with triangle ABC. Let\u2019s place BC on the x-axis, B at (0,0), C at (c,0), and A somewhere in the plane. Let\u2019s denote coordinates: B = (0,0), C = (c,0), A = (d,e). Since the triangle is acute, all coordinates will be such that the altitudes fall inside the triangle.\n\nFirst, compute the coordinates of A1, B1, C1. A1 is the foot of the altitude from A to BC. Since BC is on the x-axis, the altitude from A is vertical, so A1 = (d,0). Similarly, B1 is the foot of the altitude from B to AC. The line AC has slope (e - 0)/(d - c) = e/(d - c). Therefore, the altitude from B is perpendicular to AC, so its slope is -(d - c)/e. The equation of the altitude from B is y = [-(d - c)/e]x. The line AC has equation y = [e/(d - c)](x - c). The intersection point B1 is the solution to:\n\ny = [-(d - c)/e]x\n\nand\n\ny = [e/(d - c)](x - c)\n\nSetting equal:\n\n[-(d - c)/e]x = [e/(d - c)](x - c)\n\nMultiply both sides by e(d - c):\n\n-(d - c)^2 x = e^2 (x - c)\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n-