gles? Because I was considering the wrong vectors. Therefore, the answer might actually be yes, it is possible for any trihedral angle.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me clarify with coordinates.\n\nTake the standard octant trihedral angle with edges along x, y, z axes. Let\u2019s place point A on the x-axis at (\u03b5,0,0), B on y-axis at (0,\u03b5,0), and C on z-axis at (0,0,\u03b5). The plane passing through A, B, C is given by x/\u03b5 + y/\u03b5 + z/\u03b5 = 1, or x + y + z = \u03b5. Now, consider triangle OAB. In this triangle, O is (0,0,0), A is (\u03b5,0,0), B is (0,\u03b5,0).\n\nAngle at A (OAB): This is the angle between vectors AO and AB. Vector AO is (-\u03b5,0,0), vector AB is (-\u03b5, \u03b5,0). The dot product is (-\u03b5)(-\u03b5) + 0*\u03b5 + 0*0 = \u03b5\u00b2. The magnitudes are |AO| = \u03b5, |AB| = sqrt(\u03b5\u00b2 + \u03b5\u00b2) = \u03b5\u221a2. Therefore, cos(theta) = \u03b5\u00b2 / (\u03b5 * \u03b5\u221a2) = 1/\u221a2 \u2248 0.707, so theta \u2248 45 degrees, acute.\n\nSimilarly, angle at B (OBA) is also 45 degrees, acute.\n\nIn triangle OBC: O is (0,0,0), B is (0,\u03b5,0), C is (0,0,\u03b5). Angle at B (OBC): vectors BO (-0, -\u03b5, 0) and BC (0, -\u03b5, \u03b5). Dot product: 0*0 + (-\u03b5)(-\u03b5) + 0*\u03b5 = \u03b5\u00b2. |BO| = \u03b5, |BC| = sqrt(0 + \u03b5\u00b2 + \u03b5\u00b2) = \u03b5\u221a2. So cos(theta) = \u03b5\u00b2 / (\u03b5 * \u03b5\u221a2) = 1/\u221a2, theta \u2248 45 degrees, acute. Similarly, angle at C (OCB) is 45 degrees.\n\nSimilarly, in triangle OAC, angles at A and C are 45 degrees.\n\nTherefore, in this case, all angles OAB, OBA, OBC, OCB, OAC, OCA are 45 degrees, which are acute. Therefore, in the right-angled trihedral angle, it is possible to choose a plane section (the plane x + y + z = \u03b5) such that all the specified angles are acute.\n\nBut earlier, I thought that in the right-angled case, angles were obtuse, but that was due to a miscalculation. So actually, even in the right-angled trihedral angle, by choosing points sufficiently close to O, all those angles can be acute. Therefore, maybe it's always possible regardless of the trihedral angle's face angles.\n\nWait, but how? Let's consider a trihedral angle with one face angle very large, say 150 degrees. Let's say between OA and OB, the angle is 150 degrees. If we place points A and B very close to O, then in triangle OAB, OA=OB=\u03b5, angle at O is 150 degrees. Then, using the Law of Cosines, AB\u00b2 = \u03b5\u00b2 + \u03b5\u00b2 - 2 \u03b5\u00b2 cos(150\u00b0) = 2 \u03b5\u00b2 (1 - cos 150\u00b0). Compute AB:\n\ncos(150\u00b0) = -\u221a3/2 \u2248 -0.8660\n\nSo AB\u00b2 = 2 \u03b5\u00b2 (1 - (-0.8660)) = 2 \u03b5\u00b2 (1 + 0.8660) = 2 \u03b5\u00b2 * 1.8660 \u2248 3.732 \u03b5\u00b2\n\nAB \u2248 \u03b5 * sqrt(3.732) \u2248 1.931 \u03b5\n\nNow, compute angles at A and B in triangle OAB.\n\nUsing Law of Cosines for angle at A:\n\ncos(angle at A) = (OA\u00b2 + AB\u00b2 - OB\u00b2) / (2 OA AB)\n\n= (\u03b5\u00b2 + 3.732 \u03b5\u00b2 - \u03b5\u00b2) / (2 \u03b5 * 1.931 \u03b5)\n\n= (3.732 \u03b5\u00b2) / (3.862 \u03b5\u00b2) \u2248 3.732 / 3.862 \u2248 0.9663\n\nTherefore, angle at A \u2248 arccos(0.9663) \u2248 15 degrees, which is acute.\n\nSimilarly, angle at B is also 15 degrees. So even with a very large face angle of 150 degrees at O, the angles at A and B are acute when points are close to O. Therefore, it seems that regardless of the trihedral angle's face angles, by choosing points A, B, C sufficiently close to O, all the angles at A, B, C in triangles OAB, OBC, OAC will be acute.\n\nThis suggests that the answer to the problem is yes, it is possible for any given trihedral angle.\n\nBut wait, let's test with another example. Suppose the trihedral angle has one face angle of 170 degrees, very close to being flat. Place points A and B very close to O. Then in triangle OAB, angle at O is 170 degrees, OA=OB=\u03b5. Then AB\u00b2 \u2248 2 \u03b5\u00b2 (1 - cos 170\u00b0). Cos 170\u00b0 \u2248 -0.9848, so AB\u00b2 \u2248 2 \u03b5\u00b2 (1 + 0.9848) \u2248 2 \u03b5\u00b2 * 1.9848 \u2248 3.9696 \u03b5\u00b2, so AB \u2248 1.9924 \u03b5.\n\nAngle at A:\n\ncos(angle at A) = (\u03b5\u00b2 + 3.9696 \u03b5\u00b2 - \u03b5\u00b2)/(2 \u03b5 * 1.9924 \u03b5) \u2248 (3.9696 \u03b5\u00b2)/(3.9848 \u03b5\u00b2) \u2248 0.9962\n\nThus, angle at A \u2248 arccos(0.9962) \u2248 5 degrees, which is still acute. Similarly, angle at B \u2248 5 degrees. So even with an almost straight face angle, the angles at A and B remain acute.\n\nTherefore, the key idea is that by placing points A, B, C sufficiently close to the vertex O of the trihedral angle, the angles at A, B, C in the respective triangles OAB, OBC, OAC can be made arbitrarily small, hence acute, regardless of the face angles at O. This is because as points A, B, C approach O, the sides OA, OB, OC become very small, while the sides AB, BC, CA become proportional to the face angles but the angles at A, B, C shrink towards zero. Wait, but in the previous calculations, even with large face angles, the angles at A and B were small but positive, hence acute.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the trihedral angle's face angles, by choosing the plane section ABC sufficiently close to O, all the angles OAB, OBA, OBC, OCB, OAC, OCA can be made acute. Therefore, the answer is yes, it is possible for any given trihedral angle.\n\nBut wait, how do we ensure that such a plane exists? Because we need to choose points A, B, C on each edge of the trihedral angle such that the plane passing through them results in triangles OAB, OBC, OAC with all the specified angles acute. But as shown, by taking points very close to O, the angles at A, B, C can be made small (acute). Therefore, such a plane exists.\n\nHence, the answer should be yes, it is possible.\n\nBut wait, in the first analysis with the right-angled trihedral angle, I thought there was a problem, but that was due to a miscalculation. Actually, even in that case, by taking points close to O, all angles are acute. Therefore, the answer is yes.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that for any trihedral angle, by choosing a plane section sufficiently close to the vertex, all six angles mentioned can be made acute.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine whether a plane section \\(ABC\\) of a given trihedral angle with vertex \\(O\\) can exist such that all angles \\(OAB, OBA, OBC, OCB, OAC, OCA\\) are acute, we analyze the geometric configuration and properties of the trihedral angle and its sections.\n\n### Key Analysis:\n1. **Trihedral Angle Structure**: A trihedral angle is formed by three planes intersecting at vertex \\(O\\), creating three edges. A plane section \\(ABC\\) intersects these edges, forming triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(A, B, C\\) on the respective edges.\n\n2. **Angles in Triangles \\(OAB, OBC, OAC\\)**: The angles \\(OAB, OBA, OBC, OCB, OAC, OCA\\) refer to the angles at \\(A, B, C\\) in the triangles \\(OAB, OBC, OAC\\). For these angles to be acute, the following must hold for each triangle:\n   - In \\(\\triangle OAB\\), angles at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (i.e., \\(\\angle OAB, \\angle OBA\\)) are acute.\n   - Analogous conditions apply for \\(\\triangle OBC\\) and \\(\\triangle OAC\\).\n\n3. **Impact of Proximity to Vertex \\(O\\)**:\n   - When points \\(A, B, C\\) are chosen **very close to \\(O\\)**, the lengths \\(OA, OB, OC\\) become very small. \n   - Using the Law of Cosines, the angles at \\(A, B, C\\) in triangles \\(OAB, OBC, OAC\\) depend on the face angles of the trihedral angle and the distances of \\(A, B, C\\) from \\(O\\). As these points approach \\(O\\), the angles at \\(A, B, C\\) become small and **acute**, regardless of the original face angles at \\(O\\).\n\n4. **Example with Right-Angled Trihedral Angle**:\n   - For a trihedral angle with orthogonal edges (e.g., positive octant axes), placing \\(A, B, C\\) near \\(O\\) results in triangles \\(OAB, OBC, OAC\\) where all angles at \\(A, B, C\\) are \\(45^\\circ\\), which are acute. This contradicts initial miscalculations that assumed obtuse angles due to vector misinterpretations.\n\n5. **General Case**:\n   - Even for trihedral angles with obtuse face angles (e.g., \\(150^\\circ\\)), placing \\(A, B, C\\) near \\(O\\) ensures that the angles at \\(A, B, C\\) in the respective triangles remain acute. The Law of Cosines confirms that these angles diminish as \\(A, B, C\\) approach \\(O\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy positioning points \\(A, B, C\\) sufficiently close to the vertex \\(O\\) of any trihedral angle, the plane section \\(ABC\\) will form triangles \\(OAB, OBC, OAC\\) where all angles at \\(A, B, C\\) are acute. This holds universally for any trihedral angle, regardless of its face angles.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{Yes}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the given problem and identifying key points**: \n\nWe are given a trihedral angle with vertex \\( O \\). The problem asks whether it is possible to find a planar section \\( ABC \\) such that all the angles \\( \\angle OAB, \\angle OBA, \\angle OBC, \\angle OCB, \\angle OAC, \\angle OCA \\) are acute.\n\n2. **Conceptual approach**:\n\nWe need to consider the concept of distances and properties of isosceles triangles. If points \\( A, B,\\) and \\( C \\) are all equidistant from \\( O \\), any triangle formed with \\( O \\) will have these desired properties.\n\n3. **Choosing appropriate points**:\n\nLet's choose points \\( A, B, \\) and \\( C \\) on a sphere centered at \\( O \\) with radius \\( r \\). This ensures that each segment \\( OA, OB, \\) and \\( OC \\) is of the same length \\( r \\).\n\n4. **Formation of triangles and angles**:\n\nSince \\( A, B, \\) and \\( C \\) are on the same sphere at the same distance from \\( O \\), all angles \\( \\angle OAB, \\angle OBA, \\angle OBC, \\angle OCB, \\angle OAC, \\angle OCA \\) are angles in the bases of the formed isosceles triangles. The essential point here is understanding that an isosceles triangle with equal arms has its base angles acute.\n\nFor isosceles triangles:\n    * When sides \\( OA = OB \\), both base angles \\( \\angle OAB \\) and \\( \\angle OBA \\) must be acute.\n    * Similarly, triangles \\( OBC \\) and \\( OCA \\) maintain acute base angles.\n\n5. **Verification and conclusion**:\n\nSince all these formed triangles are isosceles with leg lengths equal, and the base angles of an isosceles triangle are always acute, it confirms that all the angles \\( \\angle OAB, \\angle OBA, \\angle OBC, \\angle OCB, \\angle OAC, \\angle OCA \\) will indeed be acute when points \\( A, B, \\) and \\( C \\) are chosen appropriately.\n\nThus, we can conclude that all specified angles will be acute if points \\( A, B, \\) and \\( C \\) lie on a circle (or equivalently, a sphere, viewed in 3D).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{yes}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that for no integer \\( n \\), the numbers \\( \\frac{n-5}{6} \\) and \\( \\frac{n-1}{21} \\) can be integers at the same time.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that there's no integer n for which both (n - 5)/6 and (n - 1)/21 are integers. Hmm, let's start by understanding what this means. If both of these fractions are integers, then (n - 5) must be divisible by 6, and (n - 1) must be divisible by 21. So, I can write these as congruences. \n\nFirst, let's express the divisibility conditions as modular equations. \n\nFor (n - 5)/6 to be an integer, n \u2261 5 mod 6. That means n can be written as 6k + 5 for some integer k. \n\nSimilarly, for (n - 1)/21 to be an integer, n \u2261 1 mod 21. So, n can also be written as 21m + 1 for some integer m. \n\nNow, the problem is to show that there's no integer n that satisfies both of these conditions simultaneously. In other words, there are no integers k and m such that 6k + 5 = 21m + 1. \n\nLet me set up the equation: 6k + 5 = 21m + 1. Let's rearrange this to solve for k or m. Subtracting 1 from both sides gives 6k + 4 = 21m. Then, subtract 21m from both sides to get 6k - 21m + 4 = 0. Hmm, maybe simplifying this equation would help. \n\nAlternatively, let's rearrange it to 6k = 21m - 4. Then, dividing both sides by 3, we get 2k = 7m - (4/3). Wait, but 4/3 is not an integer, which is a problem because 2k and 7m are integers. This suggests that 7m - 4/3 must be an integer, but 4/3 isn't, so maybe there's a contradiction here. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the rearrangement. Let me check again. Starting from 6k + 5 = 21m + 1. Subtract 1: 6k + 4 = 21m. Then, 6k = 21m - 4. So 6k = 21m - 4. Let's factor out 3 from the right-hand side: 6k = 3(7m) - 4. Divide both sides by 3: 2k = 7m - (4/3). But 4/3 is still a fraction, so 7m - 4/3 must equal 2k, which is an integer. However, 7m is an integer, so 7m - 4/3 would have a fractional part of 2/3 (since 4/3 is 1 + 1/3), which can't be an integer. Therefore, this equation has no solutions in integers k and m. \n\nBut maybe I need to approach this more formally using modular arithmetic. Let's consider the two congruences:\n\n1. n \u2261 5 mod 6\n2. n \u2261 1 mod 21\n\nIf there exists an integer n that satisfies both, then these two congruences must be compatible. To check compatibility, we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT). The CRT states that if the moduli are coprime, then there's a unique solution modulo the product. Here, the moduli are 6 and 21. But 6 and 21 are not coprime since their GCD is 3. Therefore, the CRT doesn't apply directly, and the system may not have a solution. \n\nFor a system of congruences to have a solution, the congruences must agree modulo the GCD of the moduli. So, the GCD of 6 and 21 is 3. Therefore, the two congruences must agree modulo 3. Let's check:\n\nFrom the first congruence, n \u2261 5 mod 6. Then, modulo 3, 5 mod 3 is 2. So n \u2261 2 mod 3.\n\nFrom the second congruence, n \u2261 1 mod 21. Then, modulo 3, 1 mod 3 is 1. So n \u2261 1 mod 3.\n\nBut 2 \u2261 1 mod 3 is false. Therefore, the two congruences contradict each other modulo 3. Since they don't agree modulo the GCD of 6 and 21, which is 3, there can be no solution. \n\nTherefore, there's no integer n that satisfies both conditions. Hence, it's impossible for both (n - 5)/6 and (n - 1)/21 to be integers at the same time.\n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't skip any steps. Let me rephrase it. If n \u2261 5 mod 6, then n = 6k + 5. Substitute this into the second condition: (6k + 5 - 1)/21 = (6k + 4)/21 must be an integer. So, 6k + 4 must be divisible by 21. Let's write that as 6k + 4 \u2261 0 mod 21. Then, 6k \u2261 -4 mod 21. Which is the same as 6k \u2261 17 mod 21. \n\nNow, to solve 6k \u2261 17 mod 21. We can check if 6 and 21 are coprime. GCD(6,21)=3, and 3 doesn't divide 17. Because 17 mod 3 is 2. So, in a linear congruence ax \u2261 b mod m, if GCD(a,m) doesn't divide b, there's no solution. Here, GCD(6,21)=3, which doesn't divide 17, hence no solution. Therefore, the equation 6k \u2261 17 mod 21 has no solutions. Hence, there's no integer k that satisfies this, meaning no n exists. \n\nYes, that's another way to confirm. So both approaches show that there's a contradiction, hence no such integer exists.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that there is no integer \\( n \\) for which both \\( \\frac{n-5}{6} \\) and \\( \\frac{n-1}{21} \\) are integers simultaneously, we analyze the problem using modular arithmetic and divisibility:\n\n1. **Divisibility Conditions**:\n   - \\( \\frac{n-5}{6} \\) is an integer \\(\\implies n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6}\\).\n   - \\( \\frac{n-1}{21} \\) is an integer \\(\\implies n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21}\\).\n\n2. **Contradiction Modulo GCD(6, 21)**:\n   - The moduli 6 and 21 share a GCD of 3. For the system of congruences to have a solution, the two conditions must agree modulo 3.\n   - From \\( n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6} \\), reducing modulo 3 gives \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\).\n   - From \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21} \\), reducing modulo 3 gives \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\).\n   - These results \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\) and \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\) are incompatible, as \\( 2 \\not\\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\).\n\n3. **Alternative Approach via Linear Congruence**:\n   - Substitute \\( n = 6k + 5 \\) into the second condition: \\( \\frac{6k + 4}{21} \\) must be an integer.\n   - This implies \\( 6k + 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{21} \\), or \\( 6k \\equiv -4 \\equiv 17 \\pmod{21} \\).\n   - The equation \\( 6k \\equiv 17 \\pmod{21} \\) has no solution because GCD(6, 21) = 3 does not divide 17.\n\n**Conclusion**: The two conditions \\( n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6} \\) and \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21} \\) are incompatible. Therefore, no integer \\( n \\) satisfies both \\( \\frac{n-5}{6} \\) and \\( \\frac{n-1}{21} \\) being integers simultaneously.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{No such integer } n \\text{ exists.}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Introduce the given quantities:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-5}{6} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{n-1}{21}\n   \\]\n\n2. For these quantities to be integers, it must be true that both $n-5$ is divisible by 6 and $n-1$ is divisible by 21.\n\n3. We rephrase these divisibility conditions in congruence terms:\n   \\[\n   n-5 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6} \\implies n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n-1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{21} \\implies n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n\n4. Next, we investigate whether there exists an integer $n$ that can satisfy both congruences simultaneously.\n\n5. First, express $n$ in terms of one of the congruences. From \\( n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6}\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   n = 6k + 5 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n   \\]\n\n6. Substituting this expression into the second congruence \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21} \\):\n   \\[\n   6k + 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the congruence:\n   \\[\n   6k + 5 - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6k + 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6k \\equiv -4 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n\n7. Simplify further by reducing \\(-4\\) modulo 21:\n   \\[\n   6k \\equiv 17 \\pmod{21} \\quad (\\text{since } -4 + 21 = 17)\n   \\]\n\n8. Now we need to solve the congruence \\( 6k \\equiv 17 \\pmod{21} \\). First, we determine if such a $k$ exists by checking if 6 and 21 are coprime. Since \\( \\gcd(6, 21) = 3 \\) and does not divide 17, there is no integer solution to this congruence.\n\n9. Given that there are no integer solutions for:\n   \\[\n   6k \\equiv 17 \\pmod{21}\n   \\]\n   it is clear that there is no integer $n$ that satisfies both \\( n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6} \\) and \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{21} \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{There is no integer } n \\text{ for which both } \\frac{n-5}{6} \\text{ and } \\frac{n-1}{21} \\text{ are integers.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a deck of 13 playing cards where $J, Q, K$ are valued at $11, 12, 13$ points respectively, what is the maximum number of cards that can be selected such that there are no two pairs of cards whose sums of points are equal?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. So, we have a deck of 13 playing cards, each with values from 1 to 13. The face cards (J, Q, K) are 11, 12,  \u202fand 13 points respectively. The question is asking for the maximum number of cards we can select such that no two different pairs of cards have the same sum. In other words, if I pick a subset of these cards, I need to make sure that every possible pair within this subset has a unique sum. The goal is to find the largest possible subset where this condition holds.\n\nFirst, let me restate the problem to make sure I understand it. We need to select as many cards as possible from the 13-card deck, but with the restriction that all the pairwise sums of the selected cards are distinct. So, no two different pairs can add up to the same total. That means if I have two pairs, say (a, b) and (c, d), then a + b \u2260 c + d unless the pairs are the s