\\sqrt{AD^2 - AF^2} = \\sqrt{25^2 - 24^2}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying inside the square root:\n    \\[\n    FD = \\sqrt{625 - 576} = \\sqrt{49} = 7 \\, \\text{cm}\n    \\]\n    \n    Similarly, for $EC$:\n    \\[\n    EC = \\sqrt{BC^2 - BE^2} = \\sqrt{30^2 - 24^2}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying inside the square root:\n    \\[\n    EC = \\sqrt{900 - 576} = \\sqrt{324} = 18 \\, \\text{cm}\n    \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the possible lengths of the other base $CD$:**\n\n    There are two configurations for calculating $CD$ depending on how the triangles are situated with respect to each other:\n\n    - **First configuration:**\n      \\[\n      CD = AB - (FD + EC) = 30 - (7 + 18)\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      CD = 30 - 25 = 5 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n      \n    - **Second configuration:**\n      \\[\n      CD = AB + (FD - EC) = 30 + (7 - 18)\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      CD = 30 + (-11) = 19 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the perimeter of the trapezium for both configurations:**\n\n    - **First Configuration (where $CD = 5 \\, \\text{cm}$):**\n      \\[\n      \\text{Perimeter} = AB + BC + CD + AD = 30 + 30 + 5 + 25\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      \\text{Perimeter} = 90 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n\n    - **Second Configuration (where $CD = 19 \\, \\text{cm}$):**\n      \\[\n      \\text{Perimeter} = AB + BC + CD + AD = 30 + 30 + 19 + 25\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      \\text{Perimeter} = 104 \\, \\text{cm}\n      \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nAdam and Eva can both have correct results for the perimeter of the trapezium, which are $90 \\, \\text{cm}$ and $104 \\, \\text{cm}$ respectively.\n\\[\n\\boxed{90 \\, \\text{cm} \\text{ and } 104 \\, \\text{cm}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A, B, and C can walk at 5 km/hr. They have a car that can accommodate any two of them and travels at 50 km/hr. Can they reach a point 62 km away in less than 3 hours?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem is that A, 