p the third. Alternatively, the car could shuttle back and forth. Let me think of possible strategies.\n\nOption 1: Two people start in the car, drive for some distance, drop one person off, then the driver goes back to pick up the third person. Then they meet somewhere and proceed. But this involves the car making a return trip, which takes time. Alternatively, maybe the car can drive someone partway, drop them off, and have them walk the rest, while the car goes back to get the third person.\n\nAlternatively, maybe all three start together, but the car can only take two. Wait, no, the car can only take two, so one has to walk. So perhaps the optimal way is to have two people drive ahead, drop one off, then the driver comes back to pick up the third person. Let's try to model this.\n\nLet's denote the starting point as point 0, and the destination is 62 km away at point 62. Let\u2019s suppose that two people, say A and B, take the car, and C starts walking. They drive for a certain time t1, covering distance d1 = 50 * t1. Then, perhaps A gets out of the car and continues walking towards the destination, while B drives back to pick up C. The time it takes for B to drive back would depend on how far they've gone. If they went d1 km, then the distance back to where C is would be d1 minus the distance C has walked in time t1. Wait, C has been walking for t1 hours at 5 km/h, so C has walked 5*t1 km. So when the car turns around, the distance between the car and C is d1 - 5*t1. Then, the car needs to cover that distance at 50 km/h, but since the car is going towards C, who is walking towards the destination, the relative speed is 50 + 5 = 55 km/h. Wait, no. If the car is going back towards C, and C is walking forward, then their closing speed is 50 (car) + 5 (C) = 55 km/h. So the time to meet would be (d1 - 5*t1) / 55. Let me verify.\n\nAt time t1, the car is at d1 = 50*t1. C is at 5*t1. So the distance between them is d1 - 5*t1 = 50t1 -5t1 = 45t1. Then, the car starts going back towards C at 50 km/h, and C is walking forward at 5 km/h. So their closing speed is 50 +5 =55 km/h. Therefore, the time to meet is (45t1)/55 = (9/11)t1. Let's call this time t2 = (9/11)t1.\n\nDuring this time t2, the car travels back towards C and meets C at some point. At the meeting point, the total time elapsed is t1 + t2. Let's compute where they meet. The car travels distance 50*t2 back towards the start, so from d1 =50t1, it goes back 50t2, so position is 50t1 -50t2. Meanwhile, C has walked an additional 5*t2 from their position at 5t1, so C's position is 5t1 +5t2. These two positions should be equal:\n\n50t1 -50t2 =5t1 +5t2\n\nSo 50t1 -5t1 =50t2 +5t2\n\n45t1 =55t2\n\nWhich simplifies to 9t1 =11t2 \u2192 t2= (9/11)t1, which matches the earlier calculation. So that's correct.\n\nAt the meeting point, C gets into the car, and then they drive the remaining distance to the destination. Let's compute how much distance is left after meeting.\n\nThe meeting point is at position 5t1 +5t2. The remaining distance to the destination is 62 - (5t1 +5t2). The time taken to drive this remaining distance at 50 km/h is [62 -5(t1 + t2)] /50. Let's call this time t3.\n\nMeanwhile, the person A, who was dropped off at d1=50t1, has to walk the remaining distance 62 -50t1. The time A takes to walk that is (62 -50t1)/5. This time must be less than or equal to the total time taken for the entire trip, which is t1 + t2 + t3. Similarly, the total time for B and C is t1 + t2 + t3. Since all three need to arrive within 3 hours, we need:\n\nTotal time T = t1 + t2 + t3 \u22643\n\nAnd also, the time for A to walk the remaining distance is (62 -50t1)/5 \u2264 T\n\nSimilarly, since A starts walking at time t1, the total time A spends walking is (62 -50t1)/5, and the total time T must be at least t1 + (62 -50t1)/5.\n\nWait, maybe I need to model this more carefully.\n\nLet me structure the problem:\n\n1. From time 0 to t1: A and B drive the car to distance d1=50t1. C walks 5t1 km.\n\n2. From t1 to t1 + t2: B drives the car back towards C. They meet at time t1 + t2 at position 5t1 +5t2. The distance the car covers going back is 50t2, so from d1=50t1, subtracting 50t2 gives 50t1 -50t2. This must equal C's position 5t1 +5t2. As before, leading to t2=(9/11)t1.\n\n3. From t1 + t2 to T: B and C drive the car from the meeting point to the destination. The remaining distance is 62 - (5t1 +5t2) km. The time taken is [62 -5(t1 + t2)] /50 = t3.\n\nSo total time T = t1 + t2 + t3.\n\nAlso, person A, who was left at d1=50t1 at time t1, needs to walk the remaining 62 -50t1 km. The time A spends walking is (62 -50t1)/5 hours. But since A was left at time t1, the total time A takes to reach the destination is t1 + (62 -50t1)/5. This must be \u2264 T, because T is the total time taken by B and C. Wait, actually, all three need to arrive by time T. So:\n\nFor A: t1 + (62 -50t1)/5 \u2264 T\n\nFor B and C: T = t1 + t2 + t3\n\nSo we have two equations:\n\n1. T = t1 + t2 + t3\n\n2. t1 + (62 -50t1)/5 \u2264 T\n\nBut since T is also equal to the time for B and C, we need both constraints to be satisfied.\n\nLet me substitute t2 = (9/11)t1 as found earlier. Then:\n\nT = t1 + (9/11)t1 + [62 -5(t1 + (9/11)t1)] /50\n\nSimplify the expression inside t3:\n\nt1 + (9/11)t1 = (11/11 +9/11)t1 =20/11 t1\n\nSo 5*(20/11 t1)=100/11 t1\n\nThus, remaining distance for B and C is 62 -100/11 t1\n\nTherefore, t3 = [62 -100/11 t1]/50\n\nSo total time T = t1 + 9/11 t1 + [62 -100/11 t1]/50\n\nCombine the t1 terms:\n\nt1*(1 +9/11) = t1*(20/11)\n\nSo T = (20/11)t1 + [62 - (100/11)t1]/50\n\nLet me write that as:\n\nT = (20/11)t1 + (62/50) - (100/11)t1/50\n\nSimplify the second term:\n\n62/50 = 31/25 \u22481.24\n\nThe third term: (100/11)/50 = (100)/(11*50) = 2/11\n\nSo T = (20/11)t1 +31/25 - (2/11)t1\n\nCombine the t1 terms:\n\n(20/11 -2/11)t1 =18/11 t1\n\nThus, T = (18/11)t1 +31/25\n\nNow, the constraint for A is:\n\nt1 + (62 -50t1)/5 \u2264 T\n\nCompute the left side:\n\nt1 + (62/5 -10t1) =62/5 -9t1\n\nSo 62/5 -9t1 \u2264 T\n\nBut T is (18/11)t1 +31/25\n\nThus:\n\n62/5 -9t1 \u226418/11 t1 +31/25\n\nMultiply all terms by 275 (LCM of 5,11,25) to eliminate denominators:\n\n275*(62/5) -275*9t1 \u2264275*(18/11)t1 +275*(31/25)\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n275*(62/5) =55*62=3410\n\n275*9t1=2475t1\n\n275*(18/11)t1=25*18t1=450t1\n\n275*(31/25)=11*31=341\n\nSo inequality becomes:\n\n3410 -2475t1 \u2264450t1 +341\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n3410 -2475t1 -450t1 -341 \u22640\n\nCompute constants:\n\n3410 -341=3069\n\nCombine t1 terms:\n\n-2475t1 -450t1= -2925t1\n\nThus:\n\n3069 -2925t1 \u22640 \u2192 -2925t1 \u2264 -3069 \u2192 2925t1 \u22653069 \u2192 t1 \u22653069/2925\n\nSimplify:\n\n3069 \u00f7 3 =1023; 2925 \u00f73=975\n\n1023/975 = 1.04872... approximately. So t1 \u2265 approximately 1.0487 hours.\n\nSo t1 must be at least about 1.0487 hours.\n\nNow, substitute this back into the expression for T:\n\nT = (18/11)t1 +31/25\n\nIf t1=1.0487, then:\n\n18/11 *1.0487 \u22481.6364 *1.0487\u22481.715 hours\n\n31/25=1.24 hours\n\nSo total T\u22481.715+1.24\u22482.955 hours, which is just under 3 hours.\n\nTherefore, if t1 is approximately 1.0487 hours, the total time T is about 2.955 hours, which is 2 hours and 57.3 minutes, which is less than 3 hours.\n\nBut this depends on whether t1 can be exactly such that all constraints are satisfied. Let me check if with t1=3069/2925, which is exactly 3069 divided by 2925.\n\n3069 \u00f7 2925 = 1 + (144/2925) =1 + 48/975=1 + 16/325\u22481.04923 hours.\n\nLet me compute T exactly:\n\nT= (18/11)*(3069/2925) +31/25\n\nFirst compute 3069/2925:\n\n3069=2925+144\n\nSo 3069/2925=1 +144/2925=1 +48/975=1 +16/325\u22481.04923\n\nNow compute 18/11 * 3069/2925:\n\n18*3069=55,242\n\n11*2925=32,175\n\nSo 55,242 /32,175 \u22481.716\n\nThen add 31/25=1.24\n\nTotal T\u22481.716+1.24=2.956 hours\n\nConvert 0.956 hours to minutes: 0.956*60\u224857.36 minutes. So total time\u22482 hours 57.36 minutes, which is under 3 hours.\n\nTherefore, theoretically, it is possible if they coordinate the car trips optimally.\n\nBut let's verify the distance each person travels.\n\nFor A:\n\nA rides the car for t1= ~1.049 hours, covering 50*1.049\u224852.45 km\n\nThen A walks the remaining 62 -52.45\u22489.55 km, which takes 9.55/5\u22481.91 hours\n\nTotal time for A:1.049 +1.91\u22482.959 hours\u22482h57.5m, which matches the total T.\n\nFor B and C:\n\nB starts in the car, drives 52.45 km in 1.049 hours, then drives back to meet C.\n\nThe distance between B and C when B turns around is 52.45 km -5.245 km (since C walked 5*1.049\u22485.245 km). So 52.45 -5.245=47.205 km apart.\n\nThey move towards each other at 50+5=55 km/h, so time to meet is 47.205/55\u22480.858 hours\u224851.5 minutes (t2=0.858 hours)\n\nDuring this time, B drives back 50*0.858\u224842.9 km, so position is 52.45 -42.9\u22489.55 km from start.\n\nC, during t2, walks an additional 5*0.858\u22484.29 km, so total walked by C is 5.245 +4.29\u22489.535 km. So they meet at \u22489.55 km, which matches.\n\nThen B and C drive from 9.55 km to 62 km, which is 62 -9.55\u224852.45 km, at 50 km/h, taking 52.45/50\u22481.049 hours (t3\u22481.049 hours)\n\nTotal time for B and C: t1 +t2 +t3\u22481.049 +0.858 +1.049\u22482.956 hours\u22482h57.5m, which is under 3 hours.\n\nSo all three arrive at approximately the same time, just under 3 hours.\n\nTherefore, yes, they can reach the point 62 km away in less than 3 hours.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possible mistake here. For example, when calculating the meeting point, did I account for the correct positions?\n\nAt time t1=1.049 hours:\n\n- Car (with A and B) is at 50*1.049\u224852.45 km.\n\n- C has walked 5*1.049\u22485.245 km.\n\nThen B starts driving back towards C. The distance between them is 52.45 -5.245=47.205 km.\n\nThey move towards each other at 55 km/h (car at 50 km/h backwards, C at 5 km/h forwards), so time to meet is 47.205/55\u22480.858 hours.\n\nIn that time:\n\n- B drives back 50*0.858