\) is the minor matrix obtained by removing the first row and j-th column.\n\nGiven the first row is [0, 1, 1, 1, 1], the determinant becomes:\n\n\\(0 \\cdot \\text{det}(minor) - 1 \\cdot \\text{det}(minor) + 1 \\cdot \\text{det}(minor) - 1 \\cdot \\text{det}(minor) + 1 \\cdot \\text{det}(minor)\\)\n\nBut the first term is 0, so we have:\n\n-1 * det(minor_12) + 1 * det(minor_13) - 1 * det(minor_14) + 1 * det(minor_15)\n\nSo let's compute each minor:\n\nMinor_12: remove row 1 and column 2.\n\nThe remaining matrix is:\n\nRow 2: 1, 4, 9, 5\n\nRow 3: 1, 0, 5, 9\n\nRow 4: 1, 5, 0, 20\n\nRow 5: 1, 9, 20, 0\n\nWait, actually, when removing column 2, the columns shift. Let me write them out properly.\n\nOriginal columns: 1 to 5.\n\nAfter removing column 2, the columns for each row become columns 1,3,4,5.\n\nSo Minor_12 is:\n\nRow 2: 1 (from column 1), 4 (original column 3), 9 (original column 4), 5 (original column 5)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the original matrix after removing the first row and the second column:\n\nOriginal matrix rows 2-5, columns 1,3,4,5.\n\nSo for each row from 2 to 5:\n\nRow 2: columns 1,3,4,5: entries are [1, 4, 9, 5]\n\nRow 3: columns 1,3,4,5: [1, 0, 5, 9]\n\nRow 4: columns 1,3,4,5: [1, 5, 0, 20]\n\nRow 5: columns 1,3,4,5: [1, 9, 20, 0]\n\nSo Minor_12 is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1 & 4 & 9 & 5 \\\\\n1 & 0 & 5 & 9 \\\\\n1 & 5 & 0 & 20 \\\\\n1 & 9 & 20 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, Minor_13 is obtained by removing column 3:\n\nOriginal matrix rows 2-5, columns 1,2,4,5.\n\nRow 2: [1, 0, 9, 5]\n\nRow 3: [1, 4, 5, 9]\n\nRow 4: [1, 9, 0, 20]\n\nRow 5: [1, 5, 20, 0]\n\nWait, but column 4? Wait, original columns:\n\nAfter removing column 3 (which was the third column in the original 5x5 matrix), the remaining columns are 1,2,4,5.\n\nSo for each row:\n\nRow 2: columns 1,2,4,5: [1, 0, 9, 5]\n\nRow 3: columns 1,2,4,5: [1, 4, 5, 9]\n\nRow 4: columns 1,2,4,5: [1, 9, 0, 20]\n\nRow 5: columns 1,2,4,5: [1, 5, 20, 0]\n\nTherefore, Minor_13 is:\n\n\\[\n\\begi