tween -1 and 1. This oscillation means the limit does not exist. \n\nTo confirm, we consider specific sequences approaching 0:\n1. For \\( x_n = \\frac{1}{n\\pi/7} \\), \\( \\sin\\left(\\frac{7}{x_n}\\right) = \\sin(n\\pi) = 0 \\), leading to the difference quotient approaching 0.\n2. For \\( x_n = \\frac{1}{(n + 0.5)\\pi/7} \\), \\( \\sin\\left(\\frac{7}{x_n}\\right) = \\pm 1 \\), leading to the difference quotient approaching \\( \\pm 1 \\).\n\nSince different sequences yield different limits, the overall limit does not exist. Therefore, the derivative \\( f^{\\prime}(0) \\) does not exist.\n\n\\boxed{f^{\\prime}(0) \\text{ does not exist}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. First, recall the definition of the derivative at a point $x = 0$:\n   \\[\n   f^{\\prime}(0) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0}\\frac{f(0 + \\Delta x) - f(0)}{\\Delta x}\n   \\]\n\n2. Given the function:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \n   \\begin{cases} \n   \\operatorname{arctg}(x) \\cdot \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{x} \\right), & \\text{if } x \\neq 0 \\\\\n   0, & \\text{if } x = 0\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   We substitute this into our derivative definition:\n   \\[\n   f^{\\prime}(0) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0}\\frac{f(\\Delta x) - f(0)}{\\Delta x} = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctg}(\\Delta x) \\cdot \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{\\Delta x} \\right) - 0}{\\Delta x}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the expression inside the limit:\n   \\[\n   f^{\\prime}(0) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{\\operatorname{arctg}(\\Delta x) \\cdot \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{\\Delta x} \\right)}{\\Delta x}\n   \\]\n\n4. Utilize the equivalence of infinitesimal quantities:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{arctg}(\\Delta x) \\sim \\Delta x \\text{ as } \\Delta x \\rightarrow 0\n   \\]\n\n5. Substitute the approximation and simplify:\n   \\[\n   f^{\\prime}(0) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{\\Delta x \\cdot \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{\\Delta x} \\right)}{\\Delta x} = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0} \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{\\Delta x} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n6. Examine the limit:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{\\Delta x \\rightarrow 0} \\sin \\left( \\frac{7}{\\Delta x} \\right)\n   \\]\n   The argument $\\frac{7}{\\Delta x}$ approaches $\\infty$ as $\\Delta x$ approaches $0$. The sine function oscillates between -1 and 1, so this limit does not exist.\n\n7. Conclude that the derivative does not exist:\n   \\[\n   f^{\\prime}(0) \\text{ does not exist}.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The derivative } f^{\\prime}(0) \\text{ does not exist.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nFind the maximum value of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{x^{2}+9}+\\frac{1}{x^{2}-6 x+21}+\\cos 2 \\pi x\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the maximum value of the function f(x) = x/(x\u00b2 + 9) + 1/(x\u00b2 -6x +21) + cos(2\u03c0x). Hmm, let me start by understanding the function and figuring out how to approach this.\n\nFirst, let's break down the function into its components. There are three parts: the first term is a rational function x/(x\u00b2 +9), the second term is another rational function 1/(x\u00b2 -6x +21), and the third term is a cosine function cos(2\u03c0x). To find the maximum value of f(x), I might need to analyze each component and then see how they interact.\n\nStarting with the first term, x/(x\u00b2 +9). Let me recall that for functions of the form x/(x\u00b2 + a\u00b2), the maximum and minimum can be found by taking the derivative. Let me compute the derivative of this term:\n\nLet f1(x) = x/(x\u00b2 +9). Then f1\u2019(x) = [1*(x\u00b2 +9) - x*(2x)]/(x\u00b2 +9)^2 = (x\u00b2 +9 - 2x\u00b2)/(x\u00b2 +9)^2 = (-x\u00b2 +9)/(x\u00b2 +9)^2.\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero gives -x\u00b2 +9 = 0 => x\u00b2 =9 => x=3 or x=-3. So the critical points are at x=3 and x=-3. Let me check the values here:\n\nAt x=3, f1(3) = 3/(9 +9)= 3/18=1/6\u22480.1667.\n\nAt x=-3, f1(-3)= -3/(9 +9)= -3/18= -1/6\u2248-0.1667.\n\nSo the first term has a maximum at x=3 and a minimum at x=-3.\n\nNext, the second term is 1/(x\u00b2 -6x +21). Let me simplify the denominator first. Completing the square for x\u00b2 -6x +21:\n\nx\u00b2 -6x +21 = (x\u00b2 -6x +9) +12 = (x-3)^2 +12. So the denominator is always positive, since (x-3)^2 \u22650, so denominator \u226512. Therefore, the second term is always positive, and its maximum occurs when the denominator is minimized. The minimum of (x-3)^2 +12 is at x=3, which gives 0 +12=12. Therefore, the maximum value of the second term is 1/12\u22480.0833, occurring at x=3. The denominator increases as x moves away from 3, so the second term decreases as |x-3| increases.\n\nThird term is cos(2\u03c0x). The cosine function oscillates between -1 and 1 with period 1, since the argument is 2\u03c0x. So cos(2\u03c0x) has a maximum of 1 at integer values of x (x=0,1,2,...) and a minimum of -1 at half-integers (x=0.5,1.5,2.5,...).\n\nSo putting it all together, f(x) is the sum of three components: a term that peaks at x=3 and x=-3, a term that peaks at x=3, and a cosine term that peaks at integers. To maximize f(x), we need to find x where all these components contribute constructively. But given that the first two terms have maxima at x=3, and the cosine term is 1 at integers, x=3 is an integer. So maybe x=3 is a candidate for maximum?\n\nLet me compute f(3):\n\nFirst term: 1/6 \u22480.1667\n\nSecond term:1/12\u22480.0833\n\nThird term: cos(6\u03c0)=cos(0)=1.\n\nSo f(3)= 1/6 +1/12 +1= (2/12 +1/12) +1= 3/12 +1= 1/4 +1=1.25=5/4=1.25.\n\nSimilarly, check x=0: first term is 0/(0 +9)=0. Second term:1/(0 +0 +21)=1/21\u22480.0476. Third term: cos(0)=1. So f(0)=0 +1/21 +1\u22481.0476, which is less than 1.25.\n\nx=1: first term=1/(1+9)=1/10=0.1. Second term=1/(1 -6 +21)=1/16\u22480.0625. Third term: cos(2\u03c0*1)=1. So f(1)=0.1 +0.0625 +1\u22481.1625, still less than 1.25.\n\nx=2: first term=2/(4 +9)=2/13\u22480.1538. Second term=1/(4 -12