eed A[i] = B[j]. To have this hold for all k up to L, the mappings between i and j must be consistent. Since 7 and 13 are coprime, the Chinese Remainder Theorem tells us that for each i and j, there's a unique solution modulo 91. Therefore, unless A and B are designed such that A[i] = B[j] whenever k \u2261 i mod 7 and k \u2261 j mod 13, which would require A and B to be the same sequence with period 1, which is constant. Otherwise, there must be some k where A[i] \u2260 B[j]. \n\nTherefore, the maximum L before a mismatch occurs is the minimal k where the congruence leads to a conflict. Given that 7 and 13 are coprime, the first conflict occurs at k=1*13 +1=14? Wait, in our previous example, the conflict occurred at k=14. But according to the Chinese Remainder Theorem, each pair (i,j) corresponds to a unique k modulo 91. Therefore, if we have A and B sequences designed such that A[i]=B[j] for all i <7 and j <13 that correspond to k < L, then L can be up to 91. But unless all corresponding pairs (i,j) have A[i]=B[j], which would require the sequences to be constant, L cannot reach 91. \n\nTherefore, for non-constant sequences, the maximum L is determined by the first occurrence where A[i] \u2260 B[j]. The earliest this can happen is at k=8, where k mod7=1 and k mod13=8. If A[1] \u2260 B[8], then they differ at k=8. However, if A[1] = B[8], then we check k=9: k mod7=2 and k mod13=9. If A[2] = B[9], continue. This process continues until a mismatch occurs. The maximum possible L is the number of congruent pairs (i,j) that can be maintained before a mismatch. \n\nSince 7 and 13 are coprime, the mapping from k to (i,j) covers all possible pairs (i,j) without repetition until k=91. Therefore, to maximize L, we need the maximum number of consecutive k's where A[i]=B[j]. The worst case is that they mismatch at the earliest possible k, which is 8, giving L=7. The best case is that they mismatch at k=91, giving L=90. \n\nBut since the problem asks for the maximum possible L achievable by some pair of sequences, not the minimum. Therefore, if we can construct sequences A and B with periods 7 and 13 such that A[i] = B[j] for all (i,j) pairs encountered from k=1 to k=90, then they can match up to 90 elements. However, this would require that for every i and j where there exists a k \u226490 such that k \u2261i mod7 and k \u2261j mod13, A[i] = B[j]. But this essentially defines a bijection between the residues of 7 and 13 up to LCM(7,13)=91. If we set A[i] = B[j] for all these corresponding pairs, then A and B would be the same sequence, which has a period of LCM(7,13)=91. But since A is given to have period 7 and B to have period 13, this is only possible if A and B are constant sequences. Because if they have periods 7 and 13 respectively, and they agree on all 91 elements, then their periods must divide 91, but since 7 and 13 are coprime, the only possibility is that the sequences are constant. \n\nTherefore, for non-constant sequences, the maximum initial matching L must be less than 91. The question then is, what's the maximum L for non-constant sequences with periods 7 and 13. \n\nUsing the Fine and Wilf theorem again, which tells us that if two periodic functions agree on a sufficiently long interval, then they must agree everywhere. For non-constant sequences with coprime periods, the maximum L before they must agree everywhere is p + q - gcd(p, q) -1 =7 +13 -1 -1=18. Therefore, the maximum initial matching is 18. \n\nTo verify this, suppose we have two non-constant sequences with periods 7 and 13 that agree on the first 18 elements. According to Fine and Wilf, if they agreed on 19 elements, they would have to have period 1 (be constant), which is a contradiction. Therefore, the maximum possible is 18. \n\nTherefore, applying Fine and Wilf's theorem here, the answer should be 7 +13 -1 -1=18. Hence, the maximum initial segment that can match is 18 elements. \n\nBut earlier examples with smaller periods suggested that the maximum ini