me check with the numbers we have: a=15, b=8, c=17.\n\n(a + b - c)/2 = (15 +8 -17)/2 = (6)/2 = 3. Which is equal to \u03f1. So in this case, yes, \u03f1 = (a + b - c)/2. So, this formula holds for right triangles. Therefore, another way to express \u03f1 is (a + b - c)/2.\n\nSo, if we use that formula, then \u03f1 = (a + b - c)/2. Given that \u03f1 = 3, then:\n\n(a + b - c)/2 = 3 \u2192 a + b - c = 6.\n\nBut we also know that a + b + c = 40. So, adding these two equations:\n\n(a + b - c) + (a + b + c) = 6 + 40 \u2192 2(a + b) = 46 \u2192 a + b = 23. Which is the same result as before.\n\nThen, since a + b = 23, and a + b + c = 40, then c = 40 -23 =17. Then, from \u03f1 = (a*b)/k = (a*b)/40 =3, so a*b=120. So, same equations. Thus, same solution.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the approach, we get the same system of equations leading to a and b being 15 and 8. Hence, the sides are 8, 15, 17.\n\nLet me check another example to see if the formula \u03f1 = (a + b - c)/2 holds. Take a 3-4-5 triangle. Perimeter is 12, semiperimeter 6. Area is 6, so inradius is 6/6 =1. Then, (a + b - c)/2 = (3 +4 -5)/2 =2/2=1. Correct. So that formula works. So, in general, for right triangles, \u03f1 = (a + b - c)/2. That's a useful identity.\n\nSo, in summary, for any right triangle with perimeter k and inradius \u03f1, we can set up the equations:\n\n1. a + b + c = k\n\n2. a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = c\u00b2\n\n3. a + b - c = 2\u03f1\n\nFrom equation 3, c = a + b - 2\u03f1. Substitute into equation 1:\n\na + b + (a + b - 2\u03f1) = k \u2192 2a + 2b - 2\u03f1 = k \u2192 a + b = (k + 2\u03f1)/2.\n\nSo, a + b = (k + 2\u03f1)/2 and from equation 1, c = k - (a + b) = k - (k + 2\u03f1)/2 = (2k - k -2\u03f1)/2 = (k - 2\u03f1)/2.\n\nThus, c = (k - 2\u03f1)/2.\n\nBut we also know from Pythagoras that c = \u221a(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2). So, another equation.\n\nBut perhaps using the product ab. From the area formula, ab = 2*Area, and Area = \u03f1*s, where s = k/2. So, ab = 2*\u03f1*(k/2) = \u03f1*k. Therefore, 