S} k = 465 - \\sum_{k \\in S} k = \\sum_{d \\in C} d.\n   \\]  \n\n**Conclusion**: Andrey's total consumption equals Ivan's. Hence, they drink the same amount of valerian.  \n**Final Answer**: \\boxed{\\text{Andrey and Ivan consume equal valerian drops.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the given problem where Andrey Stepanovich drinks one drop of valerian each sunny day and Ivan Petrovich drinks drops of valerian equal to the day number on cloudy days. Our task is to show that if April has an equal number of sunny and cloudy days, they both will drink the same amount of valerian drops by the end of the month.\n\n2. Let's denote the sunny days in April as \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{15} \\). Since there are 30 days in April, with half being sunny and half cloudy, we have 15 sunny days and 15 cloudy days.\n\n3. Andrey Stepanovich does not drink on sunny days, so he drinks as follows during cloudy days:\n\n- From \\( a_1 \\) to \\( a_2 \\) days (excluding \\( a_2 \\)), he drinks \\( 1 \\cdot (a_2 - a_1) \\) drops.\n- From \\( a_2 \\) to \\( a_3 \\) days (excluding \\( a_3 \\)), he drinks \\( 2 \\cdot (a_3 - a_2) \\) drops.\n- Continuing in this manner, finally, from \\( a_{14} \\) to \\( a_{15} \\) days (excluding \\( a_{15} \\)), he drinks \\( 14 \\cdot (a_{15} - a_{14}) \\) drops.\n- Finally, from \\( a_{15} \\) to the end of the month (inclusive), he drinks \\( 15 \\cdot (30 - a_{15} + 1) = 15 \\cdot (31 - a_{15}) \\) drops.\n\nHence, the total valerian drops consumed by Andrey Stepanovich is given by:\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot (a_2 - a_1) + 2 \\cdot (a_3 - a_2) + \\ldots + 14 \\cdot (a_{15} - a_{14}) + 15 \\cdot (31 - a_{15})\n\\]\n\nThis can be simplified as:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{15} i \\cdot (a_{i+1} - a_i) + 15 \\cdot (31 - a_{15}) = 15 \\cdot 31 - \\sum_{i=1}^{15} a_i\n\\]\n\n4. Now let's analyze Ivan Petrovich\u2019s consumption. He drinks valerian according to the day number on cloudy days. Therefore, the total sum of day numbers cumulated by Ivan is:\n\n\\[\n(1 + 2 + \\ldots + 30) - (a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{15})\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the first part, we get the arithmetic series sum formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{30 \\cdot 31}{2} = 465\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n465 - (a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{15})\n\\]\n\n5. Conclusively, the total drops consumed by Andrey and Ivan are:\n\n- Andrey\u2019s total consumption: \\( 15 \\cdot 31 - (a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{15}) \\).\n- Ivan\u2019s total consumption: \\( 465 - (a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{15}) \\).\n\nBoth simplify to the same aggregate:\n\n\\[\n15 \\cdot 31 - (a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{15})\n\\]\n\nThus, both Andrey Stepanovich and Ivan Petrovich drink an equal amount of valerian by the end of April.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Both drink the same amount of valerian drops.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "An arbitrary point \\( C \\) is taken on the semicircle with diameter \\( AB \\). Let \\( D \\) be the projection of \\( C \\) onto the diameter. A circle is inscribed into the shape bordered by the arc \\( AC \\) and the segments \\( CD \\) and \\( DA \\). This inscribed circle touches the segment \\( AD \\) at point \\( E \\). Prove that \\( BE = BC \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that BE equals BC in this geometry problem. Let me try to visualize the setup first. We have a semicircle with diameter AB. Point C is somewhere on the semicircle, so angle ACB should be a right angle because any triangle inscribed in a semicircle is right-angled. Then, D is the projection of C onto AB, meaning CD is perpendicular to AB. So, CD is an altitude of triangle ABC. \n\nNow, there's a circle inscribed in the region bordered by the arc AC, segment CD, and segment DA. This incircle touches AD at point E. The goal is to show that BE is equal to BC. \n\nHmm. Let me draw this mentally. AB is the diameter, C is on the semicircle, D is the foot of the perpendicular from C to AB. The inscribed circle 