 \n\nAlternatively, maybe instead of coordinates, using synthetic geometry would be better here. Let's consider properties of tangent circles and lengths. \n\nSince the circle is tangent to AD at E, AE is equal to the length from A to E along AD. Let's denote the inradius as r1. Then, the distance from E to the center of the inscribed circle is r1. Similarly, the center must be r1 distance away from CD and from the arc AC. \n\nBut how does the center relate to BE and BC? Hmm. \n\nWait, maybe coordinates are still a good approach. Let me try setting up coordinates with AB as the diameter. Let\u2019s set AB = 2r for simplicity, so A is (-r, 0), B is (r, 0), center at (0,0). Then point C is (r cos \u03b8, r sin \u03b8), D is (r cos \u03b8, 0). \n\nThe region bounded by arc AC, CD, and DA. So arc AC is from A to C on the semicircle, CD is vertical from C to D, and DA is horizontal from D to A. \n\nThe inscribed circle in this region must touch all three boundaries. Let\u2019s denote the center of the inscribed circle as O1, with coordinates (h, k). Since it's tangent to DA and CD, which are the horizontal and vertical lines. DA is the segment from A (-r, 0) to D (r cos \u03b8, 0). Wait, no: DA is from D (r cos \u03b8, 0) to A (-r, 0). So DA is the horizontal segment from D to A. Similarly, CD is vertical from C (r cos \u03b8, r sin \u03b8) to D (r cos \u03b8, 0). \n\nTherefore, the inscribed circle must be tangent to DA (the horizontal line from D to A), CD (the vertical line from C to D), and the arc AC. \n\nWait, but DA is a horizontal segment, CD is a vertical segment. The inscribed circle is tangent to these two segments and the arc. Therefore, the center of the circle must be at a distance equal to its radius from both DA and CD. \n\nSince DA is the horizontal segment from D to A, which is along the x-axis from (r cos \u03b8, 0) to (-r, 0). So DA is the line y = 0 from x = -r to x = r cos \u03b8. Similarly, CD is the vertical segment from (r cos \u03b8, r sin \u03b