t B by -60 degrees (clockwise) should give point C.\n\nCalculating the coordinates of C:\n\nFirst, translate point A by subtracting point B: (r - r cos \u03b8, 0 - r sin \u03b8) = (r(1 - cos \u03b8), -r sin \u03b8)\n\nRotate this by -60 degrees:\n\nx' = (r(1 - cos \u03b8)) cos(-60\u00b0) - (-r sin \u03b8) sin(-60\u00b0)\n\ny' = (r(1 - cos \u03b8)) sin(-60\u00b0) + (-r sin \u03b8) cos(-60\u00b0)\n\nSimplify:\n\ncos(-60\u00b0) = cos 60\u00b0 = 0.5\n\nsin(-60\u00b0) = -sin 60\u00b0 = -\u221a3/2\n\nSo,\n\nx' = 0.5 * r(1 - cos \u03b8) - (-r sin \u03b8)(-\u221a3/2)\n\n= 0.5 r(1 - cos \u03b8) - (r sin \u03b8)(\u221a3/2)\n\ny' = -\u221a3/2 * r(1 - cos \u03b8) + (-r sin \u03b8)(0.5)\n\n= -\u221a3/2 r(1 - cos \u03b8) - 0.5 r sin \u03b8\n\nThen translate back by adding point B:\n\nC_x = x' + r cos \u03b8 = 0.5 r(1 - cos \u03b8) - (\u221a3/2) r sin \u03b8 + r cos \u03b8\n\n= 0.5 r - 0.5 r cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/2) r sin \u03b8 + r cos \u03b8\n\n= 0.5 r + 0.5 r cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/2) r sin \u03b8\n\nSimilarly,\n\nC_y = y' + r sin \u03b8 = -\u221a3/2 r(1 - cos \u03b8) - 0.5 r sin \u03b8 + r sin \u03b8\n\n= -\u221a3/2 r + \u221a3/2 r cos \u03b8 - 0.5 r sin \u03b8 + r sin \u03b8\n\n= -\u221a3/2 r + \u221a3/2 r cos \u03b8 + 0.5 r sin \u03b8\n\nSo coordinates of C are:\n\nC_x = 0.5 r + 0.5 r cos \u03b8 - (\u221a3/2) r sin \u03b8\n\nC_y = -\u221a3/2 r + \u221a3/2 r cos \u03b8 + 0.5 r sin \u03b8\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Maybe there's a better approach.\n\nAlternatively, since ABC is equilateral, and A and B are on the circle, maybe we can use properties of circles and equilateral triangles. Let me think.\n\nGiven that ABC is equilateral, so angle at A and B is 60 degrees. Since A and B are on the circle, then the arc AB would subtend angle 2*60=120 degrees at the center? Wait, no. The central angle is twice the inscribed angle. If ABC is an equilateral triangle inscribed in a circle, then each central angle would be 120 degrees. But in this case, ABC is not inscribed in circle 